    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d171-a1">

                <pb facs="0352" n="312" sameAs="frus:frus1865p1/pg312"/>

                <head>Earl Russell to Mr.
                        Adams</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Foreign Office,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-04-06"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 6, 1865</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> With reference to my letter of the 10th
                    ultimo, I have now the honor to inform yon that her Majesty’s government are
                    advised that although the proceedings of the steamer Confederate States,
                    formerly Laurel, may have rendered her liable to capture on the high seas by the
                    cruisers of the United States, she has not, so far as is known, committed any
                    offence punishable by British law, and that there was nothing contrary to the
                    law of nations in placing a mail-bag on board of her for conveyance to
                    Liverpool.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, with the highest consideration, sir, your most obedient,
                    humble servant.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>RUSSELL.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
